[Event "Lichess Practice: Basic Rook Endgames: Rook vs Pawn Draw"]
[Date "2022.07.03"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Lichess Practice: Basic Rook Endgames"]
[ChapterName "Rook vs Pawn Draw"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/pqUSUw8Y/DhXGZctw"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Lichess"]
[FEN "8/1R6/8/8/8/p2K4/1k6/8 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2022.07.03"]
[UTCTime "18:19:24"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ In this interesting position, the main idea for black to be able to draw is to not obstruct the pawn on the a-file and be cut off by white's rook. Thus black always needs to put their king on the c-file if white check them with the rook on the b-file. }
1... Kc1! 2. Ra7 { White is trying to win the pawn. Black can defend it with only one move. } 2... Kb2 3. Rb7+ { White is attempting to drive black's king to the a-file once again. } 3... Kc1 { We keep moving our king on the c1 and b2 squares to keep the pawn. White hasn't made any progress. Now if white goes Ra7 and Rb7+ again, the game would end in a draw due to threefold repetition. } 4. Kc3 { Now obviously Kd1 loses due to Ra7 or Kb3 and the pawn would be lost. } 4... a2 5. Ra7 Kb1 6. Rb7+ { White is still attempting to drive the king to a1. } 6... Kc1 7. Ra7 Kb1 8. Ra3 a1=Q+ (8... a1=R { Also leads to a draw but promote to a queen to complete the exercise. }) 9. Rxa1+ Kxa1 { As you saw, white wasn't able to make any progress. } *


